      "worked_solution": "Given two ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14252285/assets/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrices ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14252285/assets/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14252285/assets/504b9ae6bd4255a78e26a2d32aaca2d613171ad01c9ac5d992f5512d093605e7.svg) we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14252285/assets/3fbf04c7a536c85422f2762eb5475ff5edfcff7a71eee0fa3cc14143e79bb10f.svg)\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14252285/assets/16c31a7e010da9d8f4e17628bc1c7b5dd3ef1d65a4e5e191bec2fd404135a7bc.svg)\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we notice that it's upper triangular. Therefore, the determinant is simply the product of the diagonal entries.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14252285/assets/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg)st column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14252285/assets/273e8879a6f78121b98902852e974aef15d9408e7cd026d3cc17e51837248ede.svg)",
